// Windows fault-disposition provider for the SEAM 3 remainder (#3337, epic
// #3322): mapping a hardware fault to the same Bit-visible panic ../linux and
// ../darwin's SIGSEGV/SIGBUS/SIGTRAP/SIGILL dispositions produce, and a
// native stack overflow to a diagnostic instead of a silent kill or a hang.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WINDOWS HAS NO SIGNALS — READ THIS BEFORE ../linux OR ../darwin's signal.bit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is a genuine redesign, not a port, exactly as the ticket says. The
// mechanism is `AddVectoredExceptionHandler`: a single, PROCESS-WIDE callback
// invoked by the OS's exception dispatcher for EVERY structured exception in
// the process (not filtered by exception code the way `sigaction` filters by
// signal number), on the FAULTING THREAD, before any frame-based (SEH/C++)
// handler gets a chance. `vehHandler` below therefore filters by
// ExceptionCode itself and returns EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH (0) for
// anything it does not explicitly own — the same discipline ../darwin/
// signal.bit's header argues for SIGTRAP (not stealing a debugger's own
// breakpoint), generalised here to every exception code this file does not
// name, since VEH has no per-code filter to lean on.
//
// NO ALTERNATE STACK, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. `sigaltstack` exists
// because a POSIX signal handler runs ON THE INTERRUPTED THREAD'S OWN
// STACK by default, which is unusable when the fault IS that stack running
// out. Windows' VEH callback ALSO runs on the faulting thread's own stack —
// there is no OS-level alternate-stack primitive for it at all — but the
// kernel reserves a small amount of extra, uncommitted stack specifically so
// a stack-overflow handler has somewhere to run (MSDN's own description of
// `SetThreadStackGuarantee`). `winInstallStackGuarantee` below asks for that
// reserve explicitly, once per OS thread, before the thread can fault.
// EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED end to end on mustafa-desktop-win (native Windows 11
// Pro, AMD64, not WSL): a `SetThreadStackGuarantee(65536)` call followed by
// unbounded recursion on the SAME thread reaches `AddVectoredExceptionHandler`'s
// callback with `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW`, and the callback successfully runs
// real code (an `fprintf`-class call, strictly more stack-hungry than this
// file's `writeConst`) and calls `ExitProcess` — not a hang, not a silent
// kill. See the per-function comments below for the exact probe.
//
// TWO WINDOWS EXCEPTION CODES, NOT DISTINGUISHED BY HEURISTIC LIKE THE OTHER
// TWO PLATFORMS. ../darwin and ../linux get exactly ONE fault signal
// (SIGSEGV, on BSD/Linux alike) for both "a green task's own guarded stack
// ran out" and "an ordinary bad pointer", so their `segvHandler` has to
// GUESS which one happened by comparing the faulting address and the saved
// `sp` against a known stack floor. Windows hands this file the answer for
// free in the ONE case that matters most: the kernel raises the DISTINCT
// `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW` (0xC00000FD) — never `STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION`
// (0xC0000005) — specifically when a thread writes into ITS OWN registered
// guard page (the TEB-tracked native stack). EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED (see
// `vehHandler`'s comment): a real overflow on a real thread delivered
// `ExceptionCode == 0xC00000FD`, not `0xC0000005`.
//
// BUT A BIT GREEN TASK'S STACK IS NOT THE OS THREAD'S NATIVE STACK, SO THIS
// FILE STILL NEEDS ../darwin's HEURISTIC FOR THAT CASE. Every green task
// (including `bit_main`'s own task) runs on a separately `mapGuardedStack`'d
// region (../boot.bit) whose guard hole is `VirtualProtect(..., PAGE_NOACCESS)`
// (../../alloc/windows/pages.bit's `protectPages`) — an ordinary inaccessible
// mapping the kernel has no notion is "a stack", NOT the special `PAGE_GUARD`
// region tied to a thread's own TEB. So a green task overrunning ITS stack
// raises the SAME generic `STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION` a bad pointer would, and
// `vehHandler` classifies it exactly the way ../darwin's `segvHandler` does:
// compare the faulting address and the saved `Rsp` against `mainStackBaseAddr`
// / `schedTaskOnStack`, with the same accepted false-negative this runtime
// already lives with on the other two platforms (#2433's guard-hole-width
// gap; not this ticket's to fix). `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW` is therefore the
// DEFENSIVE case here — the boot/worker OS thread's OWN native stack
// (`threadStackBytes`, 16 MiB) running out before any task ever switches onto
// it, e.g. a scheduler bug — not the path ordinary user recursion takes.
// Both are handled, both point at the identical `classifyOverflowTrap`
// (a straight port of ../darwin's, unchanged), because neither carries a
// faulting address that means anything as a MEMORY location — `sp` is the
// only useful signal either way, exactly ../darwin's `trapHandler`'s own
// SIGTRAP/SIGILL case.
//
// EVERY STRUCT OFFSET AND EXCEPTION-CODE CONSTANT BELOW WAS READ OFF A REAL
// RUN ON mustafa-desktop-win, NOT FROM MEMORY. This Mac has no Windows SDK to
// `offsetof` against (../darwin/signal.bit's technique), so the verification
// instead cross-compiled a probe with mingw-w64 (`x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc`,
// throwaway Docker container, removed after) against `<windows.h>`'s real
// `CONTEXT`/`EXCEPTION_RECORD`/`EXCEPTION_POINTERS`, copied the .exe to the
// box over SSH, and ran it natively:
//
//   sizeof(CONTEXT)=1232 offsetof(Rsp)=152 offsetof(Rip)=248 offsetof(ContextFlags)=48
//   sizeof(EXCEPTION_RECORD)=152 ExceptionCode@0 ExceptionFlags@4 ExceptionAddress@16
//     NumberParameters@24 ExceptionInformation@32
//   sizeof(EXCEPTION_POINTERS)=16 ExceptionRecord@0 ContextRecord@8
//   EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION=0xC0000005 EXCEPTION_STACK_OVERFLOW=0xC00000FD
//   EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION=0xC000001D EXCEPTION_BREAKPOINT=0x80000003
//
// A second probe installed a real `AddVectoredExceptionHandler` callback and
// triggered BOTH scenarios for real: a null-pointer write gave
// `code=0xC0000005 rsp=0xAA8D9FFC90 addr=0x0` (the handler's own `Rsp` read
// landed 36 bytes from a value captured immediately before the fault on the
// same thread — the offset discriminates, not vacuous); unbounded recursion
// (with `SetThreadStackGuarantee(65536)` called first) gave
// `code=0xC00000FD rsp=0xEA0C811F70 addr=0xEA0C811F90` and the handler ran to
// completion and called `ExitProcess` cleanly. Both probes, and the mutation
// (a deliberately wrong expected-value check), were scratch, not committed;
// this comment and the ticket's evidence are the durable record.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// #3704 SUSPECTED "WRONG STACK", AND THAT PREMISE IS FALSE — DISPROVED ON
// REAL HARDWARE, NOT BY CODE READING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #3704 hypothesized that a green task's compiled body does not actually end
// up running on the `mapGuardedStack` region `../boot.bit`'s `rtSpawn`/`boot`
// configure for it — i.e. a `../../sched` context-switch bug specific to
// Windows. Directly measured and REFUTED: an `asm`-read of `rsp` taken from
// INSIDE the running task (both `bit_main`'s own task and a `spawn`'d one),
// independent of this file's own classification logic, showed `rsp` tracking
// smoothly downward and staying within `[stackBase, stackBase+size)` right up
// to within one page of the guard hole, in 1-, 2- and 3-worker configurations
// on mustafa-desktop-win (native). `schedSwitch`/`schedTaskContext`/
// `mapGuardedStack` are correct here; there is no placement bug to fix.
//
// WHY THE ORIGINAL EVIDENCE POINTED ELSEWHERE: a confound that has SINCE
// LANDED, not a mystery. #3704's own measurements were taken at
// `schedStackBytes` patched down to 65536 (not the shipped 262144) and BEFORE
// `5b7d090e` (#3630, merged `aa80f8e9`) — i.e. while `compiler/pehdr.bit`'s
// `SizeOfStackCommit` was still `0x1000` against a `0x100000` reserve, so
// EVERY native OS thread's stack (boot thread and worker alike), not only a
// green task's, was only 4 KiB actually committed behind Windows' own
// lazily-growing guard page. #3630's own investigation proved, on this exact
// hardware, that a masking read landing tens of KB below `sp` on such an
// undercommitted native stack raises a `STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION` at an
// address with NO relationship to the intended memory region — an
// independently-proven mechanism fully capable of producing an unrelated
// `fromFault`/`fromSp` reading with no scheduler defect required. `main` now
// has that fix (`compiler/pehdr.bit`'s `SizeOfStackCommit == SizeOfStackReserve`,
// checked directly: `git merge-base --is-ancestor 5b7d090e main` exits 0),
// which is why re-measuring under the shipped, unpatched configuration gives
// a clean answer where the original investigation did not. The two results are
// not directly comparable; this file does not claim to know the exact
// mechanism behind the original numbers, only that the configuration that
// produced them no longer exists on `main`.
//
// The real remaining problem is dispatch, not placement, and it is NARROWER
// than "a green task's stack overflow never dispatches": an ORDINARY access
// violation on the SAME green task's stack (a deliberate null-pointer write
// in `bit_main`, plenty of headroom below `rsp`) dispatches through this
// handler and reports "bit: fatal signal: segmentation fault" correctly with
// only the default 2 OS threads (boot thread parked, one worker). Only a
// stack-EXHAUSTION fault — by definition almost no headroom left below `rsp`
// at fault time, on the native thread's own stack OR a green task's — fails
// to reach this handler at all (silent `ExitProcess`-free process death,
// raw `0xC0000005` as the exit code). #3704's own second finding (comment 2)
// describes a workaround (a third, actively-running OS thread); three
// attempts to reproduce it here (a spawned Bit task busy-looping on a second
// worker, and a raw runtime-oblivious `CreateThread` started both after and
// before worker bring-up) all still failed to dispatch, so that workaround is
// NOT confirmed reliable and nothing here depends on it. Filed back onto
// #3652, which owns the actual remaining question: why Windows does not
// deliver an exception on a thread whose current stack has no headroom left,
// and what (if anything) can be done about it from this side.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO IN THE HANDLER, PER THE TICKET'S CONSTRAINT.
// `vehHandler` and everything it calls (`classifyOverflowTrap`, the `emit*`
// functions) are `@nosplit`: no allocation, no lock this runtime might
// already hold (no lock is taken anywhere below — unlike ../darwin/../linux,
// there is no altstack claim table here, because VEH needs no altstack), and
// every path terminates via `rootExit(2)` directly — nothing here ever
// returns `EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_EXECUTION` or attempts to resume the faulting
// code. The only per-thread state this file touches is `winStackGuaranteeScratch`
// (see its own comment for why a single shared word is sound) and the
// module-scoped read-only stack-boundary state ../boot.bit already publishes
// (`mainStackBaseAddr`, `schedStackBytes` via `alignUpStack`/`alignDownStack`).
//
// Ctrl+C (SIGINT's nearest analogue, `SetConsoleCtrlHandler`) is
// DELIBERATELY NOT installed here. Matching "whatever the existing ports do
// for interrupt" literally: neither ../linux/signal.bit nor
// ../darwin/signal.bit installs ANY SIGINT disposition — both leave it at
// the OS default (terminate the process), verified by `git grep -n
// 'sigint\|SIGINT' runtime/root/{linux,darwin}` returning nothing. Windows'
// own default Ctrl+C behaviour is the same shape (terminate the process via
// `STATUS_CONTROL_C_EXIT`), so installing nothing here reproduces that
// default rather than diverging from it. Separately, `SetConsoleCtrlHandler`
// is NOT in `compiler/pesyms.bit`'s admitted Windows import surface (#3328) —
// checked directly, it is absent from `windowsImports()` — so a custom
// handler could not be added from this file alone without a shared compiler
// change, which is out of this ticket's scope; filed as #3376 if a FUTURE
// ticket wants graceful-shutdown-on-Ctrl+C rather than the current
// terminate-by-default parity.

import { schedTaskOnStack } from "../../sched"

// `LONG WINAPI VectoredHandler(PEXCEPTION_POINTERS ExceptionInfo)` (winnt.h)
// — the Win64 ABI this compilation unit already selects for every function
// (../boot.bit's header). `first: i32` is `AddVectoredExceptionHandler`'s
// `FirstHandler` — 1 so this runs before any handler a later
// `AddVectoredExceptionHandler` call might add (none exists in this runtime,
// but first-registered-runs-first is the correct posture for the one
// process-wide fault reporter). `SetThreadStackGuarantee` takes a `PULONG`
// (in/out `*i32`) — see `winInstallStackGuarantee` for why only the IN side
// is used here. Both are in `compiler/pesyms.bit`'s admitted surface (#3328).
extern fn AddVectoredExceptionHandler(first: i32, handler: *byte): *byte
extern fn SetThreadStackGuarantee(stackSizeInBytes: *i32): i32

// EXCEPTION_POINTERS (winnt.h): two 8-byte pointers. VERIFIED (see header).
const excPointersRecordOff: int = 0
const excPointersContextOff: int = 8

// EXCEPTION_RECORD (winnt.h): `ExceptionCode` is a DWORD at offset 0 —
// `vehHandler` reads it via `loadWord` (8 bytes) and masks to the low 32
// bits rather than a narrower load, exactly the way `loadWord` is already
// used everywhere else in this tree for a field smaller than a word (the
// x86-64 read is little-endian, so this is exact, not approximate). Windows
// packs the general free-form fault information at `ExceptionInformation[]`
// (offset 32, each entry 8 bytes); index 1 is the faulting virtual address
// for BOTH `EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION` and `EXCEPTION_STACK_OVERFLOW`
// (index 0 is the read/write/DEP discriminator, unused here — VERIFIED live:
// the access-violation probe's NumberParameters read back 2, matching this
// layout).
const excRecordCodeOff: int = 0
const excRecordInfo1Off: int = 40 // ExceptionInformation[1] = 32 + 1*8

// CONTEXT (winnt.h, AMD64): `Rsp` at offset 152. VERIFIED (see header).
const ctxRspOff: int = 152

// The three exception codes this file owns. VERIFIED (see header) — these
// are NTSTATUS values, not signal numbers, hence the `0xC0000000`-class
// magnitude; `int` (64-bit) holds them with no sign ambiguity, unlike
// reading them back as a narrower signed type would.
const excAccessViolation: int = 0xC0000005
const excStackOverflow: int = 0xC00000FD
const excIllegalInstruction: int = 0xC000001D // #2432/#2946's x64 `ud2` trap

// `EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_SEARCH` — hand the exception to the next handler
// (another VEH, an SEH frame, or the OS default action) unchanged. This file
// never returns `EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_EXECUTION` (-1): every code it owns ends
// in `rootExit`, and every code it does not own must be left for whatever
// (if anything) is meant to see it — a debugger's own breakpoint, first-
// chance handling, or the OS's own unhandled-exception report.
const vehContinueSearch: i32 = 0

// The floor every real stack mapping in this runtime sits above — identical
// role and value to ../darwin/signal.bit's `stackAddrFloor` (same constant,
// same purpose: reject a fault at a low, obviously-not-a-stack address
// before it can coincidentally match an uninitialized `mainStackBaseAddr`
// of 0 or a near-zero read).
const stackAddrFloor: int = 268435456

const overflowNone: int = 0
const overflowMain: int = 1
const overflowSpawned: int = 2

// Reused for BOTH `EXCEPTION_STACK_OVERFLOW` and `EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION`
// — neither carries a faulting MEMORY address (a `ud2` does not fault on
// memory, and `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW`'s own faulting address is already the
// thread's own native guard page, not a Bit stack boundary), so `sp` alone is
// the only useful signal — a straight port of ../darwin/signal.bit's
// `classifyOverflowTrap`, unchanged algorithm: `alignDownStack(sp)` lands on
// a spawned task's stack FLOOR regardless of where in the granule `sp`
// currently sits (a spawned task's whole stack fits one `schedStackBytes`
// granule), and `schedTaskOnStack` (../../sched, already `@symbol`-pinned as
// `bit_rt_port_sched_task_on_stack`) reads the tag at that floor to tell a
// live Bit stack from a coincidental address.
@nosplit fn classifyOverflowTrap(sp: int): int {
  let floorBase = alignDownStack(sp)
  if (floorBase <= stackAddrFloor) {
    return overflowNone
  }
  if (floorBase == mainStackBaseAddr) {
    return overflowMain
  }
  if (schedTaskOnStack(floorBase) != 0) {
    return overflowNone
  }
  return overflowSpawned
}

// "bit: stack overflow\n" — identical packed constants to ../darwin/
// ../linux/signal.bit's own `emitMainStackOverflow`, reused verbatim: the
// TEXT must match across all three platforms (the ticket's own acceptance
// criterion), and it is platform-independent ASCII, so the same packed words
// are correct here unchanged.
@nosplit fn emitMainStackOverflow() {
  writeConst(stderrFd, 7022364301234301282, 8) // "bit: sta"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 7382074298558606179, 8) // "ck overf"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 175599468, 4)           // "low\n"
  rootExit(2)
}

// "bit: stack overflow in a spawned task\n" — same reuse as above.
@nosplit fn emitSpawnedStackOverflow() {
  writeConst(stderrFd, 7022364301234301282, 8) // "bit: sta"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 7382074298558606179, 8) // "ck overf"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 6998715218729070444, 8) // "low in a"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 7234309835455492896, 8) // " spawned"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 11456613544992, 6)      // " task\n"
  rootExit(2)
}

// "bit: fatal signal: segmentation fault\n" / "bit: fatal signal: illegal
// instruction\n" — the first two REUSE ../darwin/signal.bit's exact packed
// constants (same text requirement as the overflow messages above). The
// third, "bit: fatal signal: stack overflow\n", is NEW: Windows' distinct
// `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW` code has no POSIX-signal equivalent for
// ../darwin/../linux to have already named, and it is reached ONLY when
// `classifyOverflowTrap` could not attribute the native-thread overflow to a
// known Bit stack (see the header — the defensive case, not the common one).
@nosplit fn emitFatalSignal(code: int) {
  writeConst(stderrFd, 8386096269746268514, 8) // "bit: fat"
  writeConst(stderrFd, 7020662571717913697, 8) // "al signa"
  if (code == excAccessViolation) {
    writeConst(stderrFd, 7308611455713032812, 8) // "l: segme"
    writeConst(stderrFd, 2336927755366659182, 8) // "ntation "
    writeConst(stderrFd, 11495152116070, 6)      // "fault\n"
  } else if (code == excIllegalInstruction) {
    writeConst(stderrFd, 7450480371430341228, 8) // "l: illeg"
    writeConst(stderrFd, 8247343735669550177, 8) // "al instr"
    writeConst(stderrFd, 2936174556767093, 7)    // "uction\n"
  } else {
    writeConst(stderrFd, 7738135737528236652, 8) // "l: stack"
    writeConst(stderrFd, 8028904877209317152, 8) // " overflo"
    writeConst(stderrFd, 2679, 2)                // "w\n"
  }
  rootExit(2)
}

// The vectored exception handler — see the header for the full contract.
// BOUNDED (Power of 10 rule 2): every path is a fixed number of loads and
// comparisons followed by a call that never returns; there is no loop.
@nosplit fn vehHandler(info: *byte): i32 {
  let recAddr = loadWord(int(info) + excPointersRecordOff)
  let ctxAddr = loadWord(int(info) + excPointersContextOff)
  let code = loadWord(recAddr + excRecordCodeOff) & 0xFFFFFFFF
  let sp = loadWord(ctxAddr + ctxRspOff)

  if (code == excAccessViolation) {
    // The generic fault path — a green task's guarded stack (the common
    // case) or an ordinary bad pointer. Same double-check as ../darwin's
    // `segvHandler`: the faulting address AND the saved `sp` must both land
    // on the same stack-granule boundary before this is trusted as an
    // overflow rather than an unrelated fault that happens to be near one.
    let faultAddr = loadWord(recAddr + excRecordInfo1Off)
    let fromFault = alignUpStack(faultAddr)
    let fromSp = alignUpStack(sp)
    if (fromFault == fromSp && fromFault > stackAddrFloor) {
      if (fromFault == mainStackBaseAddr) {
        emitMainStackOverflow()
        return vehContinueSearch
      }
      // #3651: `mainStackBaseAddr == 0` means boot() has not yet reached
      // step 5 (../boot.bit) — no green stack, main or spawned, is mapped
      // in this process. A granule-adjacent fault this early cannot be a
      // spawned-task overflow; fall through to the plain fault report.
      if (mainStackBaseAddr != 0) {
        emitSpawnedStackOverflow()
        return vehContinueSearch
      }
    }
    emitFatalSignal(code)
    return vehContinueSearch
  }

  if (code == excStackOverflow || code == excIllegalInstruction) {
    let verdict = classifyOverflowTrap(sp)
    if (verdict == overflowMain) {
      emitMainStackOverflow()
      return vehContinueSearch
    }
    if (verdict == overflowSpawned) {
      emitSpawnedStackOverflow()
      return vehContinueSearch
    }
    emitFatalSignal(code)
    return vehContinueSearch
  }

  // Not ours — a debugger breakpoint (EXCEPTION_BREAKPOINT), a first-chance
  // notification, or anything else. Hand it on unchanged.
  return vehContinueSearch
}

// Install `vehHandler` process-wide. Call once, before any worker thread
// exists (../boot.bit's step 1b) — matching where ../darwin/../linux install
// their own fatal-signal dispositions, before anything that can allocate or
// run user code.
@nosplit fn installFatalSignalHandlers() {
  let _ = AddVectoredExceptionHandler(i32(1), entryOf(vehHandler))
}

// `SetThreadStackGuarantee`'s `PULONG` in/out parameter, written once per
// call with the SAME constant (`stackGuaranteeBytes`) by every caller — the
// boot thread (../boot.bit step 1b) and every worker OS thread
// (../boot.bit's `workerBody`, once each, since the guarantee is per-thread
// and not inherited by a new `CreateThread`). MODULE STATE, NOT A LOCAL:
// `ptrOf` addresses a module cell (§11.11), the same constraint
// ../boot.bit's `winThreadIdScratch` documents. SAFE AS A SINGLE SHARED WORD
// even under concurrent worker startup, unlike `winWrittenScratch` in
// ./io.bit (which genuinely needs per-worker slots because its value is READ
// BACK and differs per call): every writer here writes the IDENTICAL
// constant, so two threads racing on this word can only ever produce that
// same value — there is no interleaving that yields a wrong result.
let winStackGuaranteeScratch: i32 = 0

// 64 KiB — the exact value EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED sufficient on
// mustafa-desktop-win for `vehHandler` (via `AddVectoredExceptionHandler`) to
// run to completion and call `ExitProcess` after a real stack-overflow
// exception on the same thread (see the header's probe). Matches
// ../darwin/../linux/signal.bit's own `sigAltStackBytes` convention (same
// granule, same reasoning: comfortably above what a minimal `@nosplit`
// handler needs).
const stackGuaranteeBytes: int = 65536

@nosplit fn winInstallStackGuarantee() {
  winStackGuaranteeScratch = i32(stackGuaranteeBytes)
  let _ = SetThreadStackGuarantee(ptrOf(winStackGuaranteeScratch))
}
